3. Quality Gates

We should have two major gates.

Gate 1 — Before Raw Acceptance

The purpose is:

"Is this file structurally trustworthy enough to enter the raw zone?"

File-level checks
File exists and is readable
Filename follows expected pattern
Required columns exist
No unexpected critical schema changes
CSV can be parsed
Timestamp column can be parsed
Expected date matches filename
Expected hourly cadence exists
Expected number of distinct timestamps = 24
No duplicate records at the expected source grain

If any critical check fails:

Landing
   │
   ├── PASS ──► Raw
   │
   └── FAIL ──► Rejected




Gate 2 — Before Analytics Publication

Now we're asking:

"Is the processed dataset trustworthy enough for consumers?"

Checks should include:

Completeness
Expected date is present
Expected hourly intervals exist
Required grids are represented appropriately
Data validity
grid_id is valid
activity measures are numeric
no unexpected negative activity
no critical nulls
timestamps are valid


Aggregation correctness

Verify that:

raw records
    ↓
country aggregation
    ↓
grid + hour aggregation

produces the expected grain:

(grid_id, timestamp)


Analytical checks
Daily totals are non-negative
Baselines are computable
Alert calculations have valid denominators
Risk scores are within expected bounds
No duplicate (grid_id, timestamp) rows

Only after these checks:

Processed
    │
    ▼
Quality Gate
    │
    ├── PASS ──► Analytics Published
    │
    └── FAIL ──► Do NOT publish

This is important: bad processed data should not overwrite trusted analytics.